# Agent guide

This repository is {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}, the `{{ .Container }}` {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}service of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an HTTP
server in TypeScript on EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an
internal Application Load Balancer{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}service of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an HTTP
service in TypeScript on Node, run as a container on Amazon ECS on Fargate
behind an Application Load Balancer{{ else if $queue }}worker of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an SQS
queue with a dead-letter queue, consumed in batches by an AWS Lambda
function in TypeScript{{ else if $schedule }}job of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an EventBridge
Scheduler schedule that invokes an AWS Lambda function in TypeScript, with
a dead-letter queue for the runs that keep failing{{ else if $site }}static site of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: a page in
TypeScript, built by Vite, served from a private S3 bucket through a
CloudFront distribution{{ else }}function of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}: an AWS
Lambda function in {{ if $go }}Go, on the AWS SDK for Go v2,{{ else if $py }}Python, on boto3,{{ else }}TypeScript{{ end }} behind an API Gateway HTTP API{{ end }}{{ if not $site }}, keeping its data
in DynamoDB{{ end }}, with its infrastructure as a CDK app in `infra/`, scaffolded by
C4 Factory with the `{{ .Standard }}` Standard. Build it to the rules below. What
it is for, and what it must never do, is in the issue you were given
and the system's needs: follow those.

## The one rule

{{ if $go -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json` and the modules in `src/go.sum`, then checks that
`go mod tidy` changes nothing, runs Prettier and gofumpt (check), ESLint with
`eslint.config.mjs` on the CDK app (typescript-eslint strict, no warnings
allowed), `go vet`, golangci-lint with `.golangci.yml`, `tsc` with the strict
`tsconfig.json`, the function's Go tests (`go test -race`), the stacks'
vitest `Template.fromStack` assertions, `cdk synth` with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- else if $py -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json` and `uv.lock` (and fails when `uv.lock` is stale), then
runs Prettier and `ruff format` (check), ESLint with `eslint.config.mjs`
(typescript-eslint strict) and ruff with `ruff.toml` (every rule), no findings
allowed, `tsc` with the strict `tsconfig.json` and mypy `--strict` with
`mypy.ini`, the function's pytest tests and the stacks' vitest
`Template.fromStack` assertions, `cdk synth` with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- else -}}
`make check` must pass before every commit. It installs the exact packages in
`package-lock.json`, then runs Prettier (check), ESLint with `eslint.config.mjs`
(typescript-eslint strict, no warnings allowed), `tsc` with the strict
`tsconfig.json`, the vitest tests (the code's and the stacks'
`Template.fromStack` assertions), `cdk synth`{{ if $site }}, which builds the site with Vite,{{ end }} with CDK Nag's AWS Solutions
{{- end }}
checks, which fail on any finding not acknowledged with a reason, and
`cfn-lint` on the templates synth wrote. CI runs the same command, and nothing
reaches `main` without it passing and a review. Never commit red.

Nothing here deploys. No command, test or script calls AWS, bootstraps an
account or needs credentials: `cdk synth` writes CloudFormation into
`cdk.out/`, offline{{ if $py }}, but for the wheels it bundles into the function, which uv
takes from its cache or, when the cache lacks one, PyPI{{ end }}.{{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }} No check builds the image either: synth only stages
its build context, and `cdk deploy` builds it, with Docker, on the deployer's
machine.{{ end }}{{ if $site }} Vite builds the site at synth, into an asset there, offline
too; `npm run dev` serves it on your machine.{{ end }}

## Workflow

1. Read the issue, its acceptance criteria, and your role guide:
   `docs/agents/implementer.md` or `docs/agents/reviewer.md`.
2. Read the code you are about to change and match it.
3. Write a failing test first: one per acceptance criterion, or one that shows the bug.
4. Make the smallest change that meets every criterion. One concern per commit.
5. `make check`, then commit with a Conventional Commit message
   (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`).
6. `AGENTS.md`, `docs/agents/` and `.c4factory.yaml` never change. Other protected files
   change only through a Standard change (see your role guide), never in a normal pull
   request. `.devcontainer/`, `.c4factory.yaml` and `.gitea/` belong to the platform.
   `Makefile`, `eslint.config.mjs`, {{ if $go }}`.golangci.yml`, {{ else if $py }}`ruff.toml`, `mypy.ini`, {{ end }}`.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`,
   `.editorconfig`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/style/`, `docs/agents/`,
   `docs/well-architected.md`, {{ if $ops }}`docs/runbook.md`, {{ end }}`infra/main.ts` and `infra/standard.ts` belong
   to the Standard. `tsconfig.json`, `vitest.config.mts` and `cdk.json` decide
   what the checks check and which CDK behaviour the app gets: don't loosen them.
{{- if $ops }}
   `infra/operations.ts` is the factory's: it renders the file from the
   Container's `spec.operations`. Change it only to what that block says,
   when an issue changes the block, and never delete it; the stacks read it.
{{- end }}
{{- if $py }}
   ruff and mypy read their own files first, so a `[tool.ruff]` or
   `[tool.mypy]` table in `pyproject.toml` changes nothing.
{{- end }}

## Lints

- Fix every finding; don't silence it. An `eslint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>`
  {{- if $go }} or a `//nolint:<linter> // <why>`{{ else if $py }} in `infra/`,
  or a `# noqa: <code>` with the reason after it,{{ end }}
  is a last resort for a false positive: one rule, one reason. Never
  `@ts-ignore`; never a file-wide disable.{{ if $py }} A `# type: ignore[<code>]`
  names its error and says why.{{ end }}
- Fix every CDK Nag finding in the stack. An acknowledgement
  (`Validations.of(construct).acknowledge({ id, reason })`) is a last resort:
  on the construct the finding's path names, for the one finding (`AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::...]`,
  never a whole rule on a stack), with a reason a reviewer can check.
- `make fmt` formats; never hand-format.
{{- if $go }}
- The function is its own Go module in `src/`, apart from `node_modules/`.
  Add a module with `go get` in `src/`, then `go mod tidy`, and commit
  `src/go.mod` and `src/go.sum`. `go build` compiles the function into one
  `bootstrap` binary, so nothing else ships. Prefer Go's standard library and
  the AWS SDK for Go v2's service modules (`github.com/aws/aws-sdk-go-v2/service/*`);
  then few, maintained, well-known modules with a licence that fits. The CDK
  app's packages are `devDependencies` in `package.json`: add one with
  `npm install --save-dev --save-exact` and commit `package-lock.json`.
{{- else if $py }}
- The function's packages are Python: add them with `uv add '<package>==<version>'`
  (`uv add --dev` for tools and type stubs), and commit `pyproject.toml` with
  `uv.lock`. The stack bundles what `uv.lock` pins, without the dev group,
  into the function, boto3 included, not the runtime's copy. Prefer the
  standard library and boto3; then few, maintained, well-known packages with
  a licence that fits, with wheels for arm64 Linux: the bundle installs
  wheels only. The CDK app's packages are npm `devDependencies`: add them with
  `npm install --save-exact --save-dev` and commit `package-lock.json`.
{{- else if $site }}
- Add packages with `npm install --save-exact` (`--save-dev` for tools), and
  commit `package-lock.json`. Vite bundles what the page imports into the
  site, so `node_modules` never ships; what the page imports is a
  `dependency`, tools and the CDK are `devDependencies`. Prefer the
  browser's own APIs; then few, small, maintained, well-known packages with
  a licence that fits. A new install script needs an `allowScripts` entry in
  `package.json`: allow it only when the package can't work without it.
{{- else }}
- Add packages with `npm install --save-exact` (`--save-dev` for tools), and
  commit `package-lock.json`. esbuild bundles what the {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}server{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}service{{ else }}function{{ end }} imports into
  one file, the SDK included, {{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}in the image's build, {{ end }}so `node_modules` never ships; what it
  imports at run time is a `dependency`, tools and the CDK are
  `devDependencies`. Prefer Node's own modules and the AWS SDK v3's modular
  clients (`@aws-sdk/client-*`, `@aws-sdk/lib-dynamodb`); then few, maintained,
  well-known packages with a licence that fits. A new install script needs an
  `allowScripts` entry in `package.json`: allow it only when the package can't
  work without it.
{{- end }}

## Review rules (lint can't check these)

{{ if $site -}}
1. **The page is plain TypeScript:** `index.html` holds the markup, and
   `src/main.ts` only finds its elements and wires their events to pure
   functions. A missing element fails at load, with its selector, not later.
2. **Accessibility:** semantic elements (`button`, `a`, `label`, headings in
   order), every input labelled, images with `alt`, everything usable by
   keyboard with visible focus, status messages in an `aria-live` region
   (WCAG 2.2 AA).
3. **Errors users see** are on the page and say what happened and what to do;
   never only in the console. A failed request leaves the page usable.
4. **Never a secret in the site:** everything Vite builds, and everything in
   `public/`, is public. Untrusted input (the URL, a form, a fetched answer)
   is written as text (`textContent`), never as HTML (`innerHTML`) or into a
   `javascript:` URL.
5. **Input:** empty, huge, malformed and non-ASCII input never breaks the
   page.
6. **The site's files:** what the page loads is imported from `src/`, which
   Vite bundles under hashed names, or is in `public/`, copied as it is and
   referenced by its path. A deploy replaces every file in the bucket: nothing
   is put there any other way.
7. **Types:** no `any` (narrow `unknown` at the boundary, checked at runtime when
   the input is untrusted); no non-null assertion without a disable comment
   saying why; no `as` to silence the compiler; exhaustive switches over unions.
8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that don't touch the DOM, tested
   with plain vitest in Node; `src/main.ts` only wires them to the page. A
   test that needs the DOM brings its own environment.
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo.test.ts`), `it.each` tables, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests.
   No test calls AWS: the stack is checked with `Template.fromStack`
   assertions.
{{- else -}}
{{ if $go -}}
1. **Cold start:** the SDK's configuration and clients are created once, in
   `main` in `src/main.go` before `lambda.Start`, and reused by every
   invocation; nothing heavy runs in `init`. The binary imports only the
   service modules it uses.
{{- else if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" -}}
1. **Start-up and shutdown:** SDK clients and configuration are created
   once, in `src/main.ts`, and reused by every request; a missing setting
   stops the server at start-up, not at the first request. On `SIGTERM` the
   server finishes the requests in flight and exits. The bundle imports only
   the clients it uses.
{{- else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" -}}
1. **Start and stop:** SDK clients and configuration are created once, in
   `src/main.ts`, and reused by every request; nothing heavy runs per
   request that could run once. On `SIGTERM` the server stops taking
   requests and finishes those in flight. The bundle imports only the
   clients it uses.
{{- else if $py -}}
1. **Cold start:** boto3 clients, resources and configuration are created
   once, at module scope in `app/handler.py`, and reused by every invocation;
   nothing heavy runs at import beyond that.
{{- else -}}
1. **Cold start:** SDK clients and configuration are created once, at module
   scope in `src/handler.ts`, and reused by every invocation; nothing heavy
   runs at import beyond that. The bundle imports only the clients it uses.
{{- end }}
{{ if $queue -}}
2. **The boundary:** every message is validated before use (its body, its
   attributes), with unknown input narrowed from `unknown`, whoever sent it.
   A message that fails throws, so the batch reports it alone and SQS
   delivers it again; one no retry can fix throws too, and waits in the
   dead-letter queue for a person. Never swallow a failure: a message the
   handler drops is lost.
{{- else if $schedule -}}
2. **The boundary:** every event is validated before use (its scheduled
   time, its attempt number), with unknown input narrowed from `unknown`,
   whoever invoked the function. A run that fails throws, so Lambda retries
   it and then sends it to the dead-letter queue; one no retry can fix
   throws too, and waits there for a person. Never swallow a failure: a run
   the handler drops is lost.
{{- else -}}
2. **The boundary:** every request is validated before use (path, query,
   body, headers), with {{ if $py }}untrusted input narrowed from `object` with `isinstance`{{ else }}unknown input narrowed from `unknown`{{ end }}. Errors are
   answered with the right HTTP status (400 for bad input, 404, 409, 500 for
   a bug) and a JSON message a client can act on, never a stack trace or the
   SDK's error text.
{{- end }}
3. **Idempotency:** {{ if $schedule }}a run delivered again{{ else }}a retried request or event{{ end }} changes nothing twice. Writes
   are puts of a whole item, conditional writes, or keyed by an idempotency
   key; never a blind increment.{{ if $schedule }} The job's work goes in `runJob` in `src/job.ts`,
   which the handler runs through `checkingFirst` and Powertools' Idempotency,
   keyed by the scheduled time, so a run delivered twice runs once; never
   bypass them.{{ end }}
{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}4. **Limits are set:** the server caps what it reads (body size) and keeps
   idle connections open longer than the load balancer's 60 seconds; the
   instance type, the Auto Scaling group's minimum and maximum and its scaling
   policy are set in the stack. A request is answered within the load
   balancer's idle timeout; SDK calls stay within it.{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}4. **Limits are set:** the task's CPU and memory and the service's scaling
   bounds are set in the stack for the work; every request body has a
   limit; keep-alive outlasts the load balancer's idle timeout, and the
   container's stop timeout leaves the requests in flight time to finish.{{ else if $queue }}4. **Limits are set:** the function's timeout and memory, the batch size,
   the event source's maximum concurrency, and the queue's visibility
   timeout (six times the function's, plus the batching window) and redrive count are set in the
   stack; SDK calls stay within the timeout.{{ else if $schedule }}4. **Limits are set:** the function's timeout and memory, the schedule's
   expression and retry policy, and Lambda's retries and maximum event age
   are set in the stack; a run finishes within the timeout, and SDK calls
   stay within it.{{ else }}4. **Limits are set:** every function has its timeout and memory set in the
   stack, the timeout below API Gateway's 30 seconds and above what the work
   needs; SDK calls stay within it. The stage is throttled.{{ end }}
5. **Logs:** structured JSON, one entry per line, through the {{ if $go }}`*slog.Logger`{{ else }}`Log`{{ end }} the
   code is given; an entry names the {{ if $queue }}message id{{ else if $schedule }}scheduled time{{ else }}request id{{ end }} and what failed. Never
   log a secret, a token or {{ if $queue }}a message{{ else if $schedule }}an event{{ else }}a request{{ end }} body with user data.
6. **Least privilege:** permissions come from CDK grants
   ({{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}`table.grant(taskDefinition.taskRole, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`{{ else }}`table.grant(fn, 'dynamodb:GetItem')`{{ end }}, `grantReadData`), narrowed to the
   calls the code makes; never an IAM statement with `*` or `service:*`
   actions, never an AWS managed policy on a role.
{{- if $go }}
7. **Errors and context:** every error is handled or returned, wrapped with
   `%w` and what was being done; the invocation's `ctx`, which carries
   Lambda's deadline, goes to every SDK call. No `panic`, `log.Fatal` or
   `os.Exit` past start-up in `main`; no goroutine left running when the
   handler returns. In the CDK app: no `any`, no `as` to silence the
   compiler, exhaustive switches over unions.
8. **Pure code:** the rules live in files that import neither the AWS SDK
   nor `aws-lambda-go`, tested with plain `go test`; one type wraps each AWS
   service behind an interface of the client methods it calls, which tests
   fake; `src/main.go` only wires them.
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo_test.go`), table-driven, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests.
   No test calls AWS: SDK clients are faked behind their interface, and
   stacks are checked with `Template.fromStack` assertions.
{{- else if $py }}
7. **Types:** no `Any` (narrow `object` at the boundary, checked at runtime
   when the input is untrusted); no `cast` to silence mypy outside tests'
   fakes; `TypedDict` for the event and response shapes; `match` over a
   closed set ends in `assert_never`. TypeScript in `infra/` follows
   `docs/style/typescript.md`: no `any`, no `as` to silence the compiler.
8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that don't import boto3, tested
   with plain pytest; one module wraps each AWS service behind a `Protocol`
   the tests fake; `app/handler.py` only wires them.
9. **Tests:** pytest functions in `tests/test_*.py`, `@pytest.mark.parametrize`
   tables with `ids`, names that say what is expected; no real timers,
   network or shared state between tests. No test calls AWS: boto3 is faked
   behind its `Protocol`, and stacks are checked with `Template.fromStack`
   assertions.
{{- else }}
7. **Types:** no `any` (narrow `unknown` at the boundary, checked at runtime when
   the input is untrusted); no non-null assertion without a disable comment
   saying why; no `as` to silence the compiler; exhaustive switches over unions.
8. **Pure modules:** logic lives in modules that import neither the AWS SDK
   {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}nor `node:http`, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each AWS
   service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/server.ts` adapts HTTP to
   them and `src/main.ts` only wires them.{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}nor Node's HTTP server, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each
   AWS service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/main.ts` only
   wires them.{{ else }}nor the `aws-lambda` types, tested with plain vitest; one module wraps each
   AWS service behind an interface the tests fake; `src/handler.ts` only
   wires them.{{ end }}
9. **Tests:** next to the code (`foo.test.ts`), `it.each` tables, names that say
   what is expected; no real timers, network or shared state between tests{{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }};
   no test starts the server{{ end }}.
   No test calls AWS: SDK clients are stubbed behind their interface, and
   stacks are checked with `Template.fromStack` assertions.
{{- end }}
{{- end }}
10. **Performance:** when the issue or the system's needs state a budget, a test
    or benchmark measures it on realistic input and the pull request gives the
    numbers.
11. **The stack:** data is encrypted at rest, every log group has a retention,
    every stateful resource an explicit removal policy (`RETAIN` for data), and
    every stack the Standard's tags; `infra/standard.test.ts` checks these. No
    secret in {{ if $site }}the site or the template.{{ else }}an environment variable or the template: secrets are read from
    Secrets Manager or Parameter Store at run time{{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}, or passed as ECS `secrets`{{ end }}.{{ end }} Nothing in the app needs
    an AWS account to synthesize: no lookups, no `env` from the environment.
{{- if $site }}
    The stack is built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which a
    deploy names (`-c tier=production`) and `infra/standard.ts`'s
    `TIER_RULES` defines, and keeps what `infra/operations.ts` promises: in
    production the site's bucket kept and versioned, its earlier versions
    expiring after a set time, and a web application firewall; the
    distribution and the function that copies the site with their alarms on
    the stack's topic; the `EntryPoint` and `AlarmTopicArn` outputs.
    `infra/standard.test.ts` checks both tiers. Nothing at synth reads a
    context value but `tier`, or an environment variable. A change to what
    the stack keeps, or how, updates `docs/runbook.md`, as a Standard change.
{{- end }}
{{- if $tiered }}
    The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which a
    deploy names (`-c tier=production`) and `infra/standard.ts`'s
    `TIER_RULES` defines, and keep what `infra/operations.ts` promises: data
    kept, protected and backed up in production; every {{ if eq .Framework "aws-fargate-ts" }}load balancer,
    target group, service and table{{ else }}function, table and
    {{ if or $queue $schedule }}dead-letter queue{{ else }}API{{ end }}{{ end }} with its alarms on the stack's topic; every stack with its
    `EntryPoint` and `AlarmTopicArn` outputs. `infra/standard.test.ts` checks
    both tiers. Nothing at synth reads a context value but `tier`, or an
    environment variable. A change to what the stacks keep, or how, updates
    `docs/runbook.md`, as a Standard change.
{{- end }}
12. **Releases:** user-visible changes get a `CHANGELOG.md` entry under
    "Unreleased"; the version in `{{ if $py }}pyproject.toml{{ else }}package.json{{ end }}` changes only in a release issue.
{{- if $go }}
13. **Names:** Go's: `mixedCaps`,
    initialisms in one case (`ID`, `HTTPURL`), files `snake_case.go`. The CDK
    app's TypeScript: `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types,
    `CONSTANT_CASE` module constants, files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- else if $py }}
13. **Names:** Python: `snake_case` functions and values, `PascalCase`
    classes, `UPPER_CASE` module constants, files `snake_case.py`; the event's
    own keys keep AWS's spelling (`routeKey`). The CDK app's TypeScript:
    `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types, files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- else }}
13. **Names:** `lowerCamelCase` values, `UpperCamelCase` types, `CONSTANT_CASE`
    module constants, acronyms as words (`HttpUrl`); files `snake_case.ts`.
{{- end }}
14. **No dead code:** no commented-out code, speculative options or "for later"
    features; no TODO without an issue number.

